# ARIA (RFC 5794, A Description of the ARIA Encryption Algorithm, 2010): a
# 128-bit block cipher with 128-, 192- or 256-bit keys (12, 14 or 16
# rounds), as a cipher for cipher-modes' modes (~LcAriaKey,
# ~LcAria.encrypt, ~LcAria.decrypt).
#
# Each round but the last is the round key XORed in, a substitution layer
# (SL1 in odd rounds, SL2 in even ones) and the diffusion layer A; the last
# is the round key, SL2 and the final round key. SL1 applies SB1, SB2, SB3,
# SB4 to the bytes in turn and SL2 applies SB3, SB4, SB1, SB2; SB3 and SB4
# are the inverses of SB1 and SB2, so SL2 undoes SL1 and SL1 undoes SL2.
#
# The cipher works on the block's 16 bytes, each kept as its eight bits
# (LcAriaSt), and the key holds it as a list of steps. Decryption undoes the
# steps last to first: a round key by XOR, SL1 by SL2 and SL2 by SL1, A by
# its elementary steps undone in reverse order (gen/tables.py writes A as a
# chain of steps x_a ^= x_b). That is the same function as RFC 5794's
# decryption with the keys dk (A is an involution), and it makes
# decrypt(encrypt(b)) == b provable for every key and block: each S-box and
# its inverse are checked on all 256 bytes, and every other step is undone
# by XOR. The S-boxes are decision trees on a byte's bits (no table is built
# at run time); the key schedule works on whole words.
